From 2021020d2eedcb2a3c37de3483062ef5f18dfd71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:29:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Confirm annotation reads after source events; wait
 with poll

Review of PR #4042 (t_d384b51aed5eea33) found two behavior defects.

R1: a WAL commit could be missed. SQLite appends the commit frames to
-wal (the inotify event), fsyncs under synchronous=FULL, and only then
publishes the commit through the memory-mapped -shm index, which emits
no event. A read answering the notification inside that window sees the
previous revision, was marked settled, and nothing woke the holder
again. Measured in the new regression: the event-triggered read returned
the old board revision 7-18 ms after the commit in most iterations.

Fix: a read that answers a source invalidation (source event, overflow,
watch loss, new ownership, explicit wake) is followed by bounded one-shot
confirmation re-reads at +0.25 s and +HOLDER_INTERVAL_S. Confirmations
never schedule further confirmations, a newer invalidation replaces the
pending ones instead of stacking, and demand-only wakes do not consume
them, so an idle source is still unread. A scope whose read failed or was
busy keeps its unsettled mark until a read succeeds.

R2: select.select raises ValueError for valid descriptors >= 1024, which
escaped the event loop and ended the holder. The wait now uses
select.poll; an error/hangup mask raises OSError so the loop's existing
recovery reinstalls coverage.

Tests: real WAL writer through the real holder and projection, 24
commits each required to project with no wake/request/unrelated event,
then 3 s without a read; frozen-clock unit test of confirmation
scheduling and coalescing; real inotify and wake-pipe descriptors
duplicated above 1100. Each fails under its mutant (confirmations off:
"commit N never projected"; select restored: ValueError). Existing
native-holder cases now detect transitions by changed output rather than
"any second read", and the loss/overflow cases wait out the initial
confirmations so their re-read is attributable to the loss.
